  "memory_summary": "The player asks Old Men to describe the Boat House, and he answers with a nostalgic, weathered account of the place.",
  "new_entities": [],
  "rule_flags": [],
  "narration": "Old Men leans back in his chair, one hand resting on a chipped wooden armrest. The creak of the floorboards answers him as he shifts his weight, and he looks around the room with a fond, distant gaze — as if the walls themselves are old friends.",
  "npc_response": "This place? It's been here longer than most of the men who drink here. It was built for the boats, you know — the ones that left before any of us were born. The walls have heard the oars, the shouts, the good times and the bad. I've sat in this chair since I was a boy, and I've watched every tide come in and go out. It's not much, but it's honest. And the fish on the wall — they were caught by men who knew the sea better than I ever will. I like to think they're still watching, the way they do now."
}

