{
  "world_updates": [
    {
      "type": "NPC_SPOKE"
    }
  ],
  "memory_summary": "Vorn deflects the player's question about conversation with another paranoid, cryptic monologue, pressing the same trust test.",
  "new_entities": [],
  "rule_flags": [],
  "narration": "Vorn's eyes narrow as he leans back against a shelf of tomes, one hand drifting toward the warded box. The dust motes swirl faster around him for a moment, as if the room itself is listening.",
  "npc_response": "People talk about the weather, the same as always. But the weather here is not wind or rain—it is the slow unraveling of the city itself. They talk about the Silk Cloaks, about the bargains they strike in candlelight. They talk about the wanderers who come asking for answers and leave with questions. And they talk about the silence. The silence is the oldest thing in this room, and it is listening. You ask what people talk about here? They talk about you. They talk about the hundred questions you carry and the answers you have not yet earned. And I... I talk about the wound that stitched the wards while the city bled. So tell me, wanderer—what do you know that I do not? Because if you know nothing, then you are already dead to me."
}

{
  "world_updates": [
    {
      "type": "NPC_SPOKE"
    }
  ],
  "memory_summary": "Vorn refuses to answer the gift question, spiraling into paranoia about the player's identity and the unraveling magic.",
  "new_entities": [],
  "rule_flags": [],
  "narration": "Vorn's eyes snap toward the twisted tree, then back to the player, his voice dropping to a whisper before rising into a frantic crescendo.",
  "npc_response": "A gift? You think I would ask for a gift from a wanderer with a blood-stained ledger? You have not brought me anything, and you never will. But I know what you promised — I can hear it in the silence between your words, like the god's name bleeding through the cracks. You promised to bring me the thing that unravels the stitches. The shard. The piece of the shattered god that you took from the puzzle box. And you have not brought it. You have only brought yourself, and that is not enough. The ravens know what you promised. They have been counting your footsteps since the third night. Do not tell me you forgot. You do not forget what you promised. You only forget when the world tries to make you forget."
}

